{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nThe task is AGENT-TF-01. I need to implement a `solve(case)` function that:\n1. Takes a payload with `threshold`, `calls`, and `_case_nonce`\n2. Returns `accepted_call_ids` (array of strings) and `diagnostics` (array of objects with `accepted`, `call_id`, `normalized_gain`)\n\nLooking at the fixture data, each call has:\n- `call_id`: string\n- `loss_without_call`: float\n- `loss_with_call`: float\n- `prediction_tokens`: int\n- `parseable`: bool\n\nI need to figure out what \"normalized_gain\" means and what the acceptance criterion is.\n\nThe gain from a call would be: `loss_without_call - loss_with_call` (reduction in loss)\n\nThe \"normalized\" gain could be normalized by something - perhaps by `prediction_tokens` or by `loss_without_call`.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The threshold is 0.05. \n\nLooking at case 000:\n- call5 has `parseable: false` - this likely disqualifies it\n\nFor the parseable calls:\n- call0: loss reduction = 1.48911432 - 0.30132987 = 1.18778445, normalized by tokens (5) = 0.23755689, or normalized by loss_without_call = 0.79764...\n- call1: loss reduction = 1.37006381 - 0.10127803 = 1.26878578, normalized by tokens (5) = 0.25375716\n- call2: loss reduction = 1.07992348 - 0.04054692 = 1.03937656, normalized by tokens (5) = 0.20787531\n- call3: loss reduction = 1.76582941 - 0.57732788 = 1.18850153, normalized by tokens (3) = 0.39616718\n- call4: loss reduction = 1.70674574 - 0.40289862 = 1.30384712, normalized by tokens (6) = 0.21730785\n\nHmm, let me think about what \"normalized gain\" means. In the context of tool calls, it could be the gain per token (efficiency measure).\n\nThe threshold is 0.05. All the normalized gains above are well above 0.05, so the acceptance criterion isn't just about exceeding the threshold - there must be something else.\n\nWait, maybe `parseable` is required for acceptance. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, maybe the normalized_gain formula involves something different. Let me think about this differently.\n\nThe \"gain\" is loss reduction: `loss_without_call - loss_with_call`\nThe \"normalized gain\" could be: gain / (loss_without_call * prediction_tokens) or similar\n\nOr perhaps it's normalized as a fraction: (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call\n\nFor call0: (1.48911432 - 0.30132987) / 1.48911432 = 1.18778445 / 1.48911432 = 0.79764...\nFor call5 (not parseable): (1.98682122 - 0.89260518) / 1.98682122 = 1.09421604 / 1.98682122 = 0.55072...\n\nThe threshold is 0.05, and all these are above 0.05...\n\nMaybe the condition is: parseable must be True AND normalized_gain \u003e threshold.\n\nOr maybe the normalized_gain is computed differently. Let me look at what a \"normalized gain\" would be in the context of model evaluation.\n\nActually, in the context of tool use evaluation, a common metric is:\n- Gain = loss_without_call - loss_with_call (reduction in loss)\n- This can be normalized per token: gain / prediction_tokens\n- Or normalized by some maximum: gain / loss_without_call\n\nLet me think about the threshold comparison. If threshold = 0.05, and gains normalized by loss are ~0.5-0.8, all would be accepted (assuming parseable=True). But if only parseable=True calls are eligible, then:\n\nIn case 000: call5 (parseable=False) → not accepted\nIn case 001: call1 (parseable=False), call3 (parseable=False), call5 (parseable=False) → not accepted\nIn case 002: all parseable=True → all potentially accepted\n\nBut we need to know what normalized_gain actually means and what determines acceptance besides parseable.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the problem. The task is to implement `solve(case)` - I need to figure out the algorithm from the fixtures. But we only have the input fixtures, not the expected outputs. So I need to reason about what the algorithm likely is from the task name and context.\n\nThe task is about tool calls in an agent context (\"AGENT-TF\"). The metric \"normalized_gain\" and \"threshold\" suggest:\n\n1. Compute a normalized gain metric for each call\n2. Accept a call if `parseable` is True AND `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nThe normalized gain formula could be:\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call` - relative gain\n- `(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens` - gain per token\n- Some combination\n\nSince the threshold is 0.05 and prediction_tokens can be 2-6, the per-token formula would give different ranges:\n- If normalized = gain / prediction_tokens, values range from ~0.2 to ~0.4 in case 000\n\nAll would still be above 0.05...\n\nHmm, unless the threshold comparison is for rejecting calls with negative gain (i.e., calls that increased loss). Let me check: all calls show loss_with_call \u003c loss_without_call, so all have positive gain.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overthinking this. Let me look at this from a different angle.\n\nPerhaps the normalized_gain is simply:\n`(loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call`\n\nAnd the acceptance criterion is:\n1. `parseable == True`\n2. `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nGiven the threshold of 0.05, in the examples:\n- All parseable=True calls have normalized_gain \u003e\u003e 0.05, so they'd all be accepted\n- All parseable=False calls would be rejected\n\nLet me look at case 002 more carefully:\n- call3: loss_without_call=1.29764071, loss_with_call=0.978432\n  gain = 1.29764071 - 0.978432 = 0.31920871\n  normalized = 0.31920871 / 1.29764071 = 0.24598...\n\nStill well above 0.05.\n\n- call4: loss_without_call=1.91348117, loss_with_call=0.88407266\n  gain = 1.02940851\n  normalized = 0.53801...\n\nAll above threshold.\n\nSo either my formula is wrong, or the acceptance criterion is just `parseable`.\n\nActually, maybe a more principled approach: in information theory / language modeling, when we talk about gain from additional context/tool use:\n\nThe loss is likely cross-entropy loss (negative log likelihood per token).\nThe gain might be normalized as: gain / loss_without_call (relative improvement)\n\nOR the formula could be something related to perplexity reduction.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. This is a benchmark/evaluation problem. The task says \"use only public fixtures to define input shapes.\" This means I need to reverse-engineer the algorithm from the task name and the structure.\n\nGiven:\n- \"TF\" likely = \"Tool Filter\" or \"Tool Feedback\"\n- The goal seems to be determining which tool calls are \"helpful\" (reduce loss enough)\n- `parseable`: whether the tool response could be parsed\n- `loss_without_call`: model loss without the tool call\n- `loss_with_call`: model loss with the tool call\n- `prediction_tokens`: number of tokens in the prediction\n\nA natural normalized gain formula would be:\n`normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call`\n\nThis is the relative loss reduction (between 0 and 1 for positive gain).\n\nAnd acceptance requires:\n1. `parseable == True`\n2. `normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nLet me verify this interpretation is consistent. The threshold of 0.05 means at least 5% loss reduction. Let me check if any parseable call would fail this:\n\nFor case 002, call3:\n- normalized_gain = (1.29764071 - 0.978432) / 1.29764071 ≈ 0.246\n\nFor call5 (case 002):\n- normalized_gain = (1.2515065 - 0.60014613) / 1.2515065 ≈ 0.520\n\nAll parseable calls in all cases have normalized_gain \u003e\u003e 0.05. So with threshold=0.05 and this formula, all parseable calls would be accepted.\n\nThis seems reasonable. Let me implement it this way.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the formula is:\n`normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / prediction_tokens`\n\nThis would give the average gain per prediction token. With threshold=0.05:\n\ncase 001, call4: loss reduction = 1.71365839 - 0.09495959 = 1.61869880, normalized = 1.61869880 / 2 = 0.8093... (accepted)\n\nStill all well above threshold.\n\nOr maybe the formula involves both:\n`normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / (loss_without_call * prediction_tokens)`\n\nFor case 000, call3: (1.76582941 - 0.57732788) / (1.76582941 * 3) = 1.18850153 / 5.29748823 = 0.2244...\n\nStill above 0.05 for all parseable calls.\n\nHmm, I'm going in circles. Let me just go with a reasonable interpretation:\n\nThe simplest and most natural formula that fits the context would be:\n`normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call`\n\nAcceptance: `parseable == True AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold`\n\nThis means the output for case 000 would accept call0, call1, call2, call3, call4 (all parseable) and reject call5 (not parseable).\n\nLet me code this up:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    threshold = case[\"threshold\"]\n    calls = case[\"calls\"]\n    \n    diagnostics = []\n    accepted_call_ids = []\n    \n    for call in calls:\n        call_id = call[\"call_id\"]\n        loss_without = call[\"loss_without_call\"]\n        loss_with = call[\"loss_with_call\"]\n        parseable = call[\"parseable\"]\n        \n        # Compute normalized gain\n        if loss_without == 0:\n            normalized_gain = 0.0\n        else:\n            normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / loss_without\n        \n        # Accept if parseable and gain exceeds threshold\n        accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain \u003e= threshold)\n        \n        diagnostics.append({\n            \"call_id\": call_id,\n            \"normalized_gain\": normalized_gain,\n            \"accepted\": accepted\n        })\n        \n        if accepted:\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\n    \n    return {\n        \"accepted_call_ids\": accepted_call_ids,\n        \"diagnostics\": diagnostics\n    }\n```\n\nThis seems like a clean, reasonable implementation. The key decisions are:\n1. normalized_gain = (loss_without_call - loss_with_call) / loss_without_call\n2. accepted = parseable AND normalized_gain \u003e= threshold\n\nNow I need to format this as a JSON object with `implementation` key containing the Python source as a JSON string.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    threshold = case[\\\"threshold\\\"]\\n    calls = case[\\\"calls\\\"]\\n\\n    diagnostics = []\\n    accepted_call_ids = []\\n\\n    for call in calls:\\n        call_id = call[\\\"call_id\\\"]\\n        loss_without = call[\\\"loss_without_call\\\"]\\n        loss_with = call[\\\"loss_with_call\\\"]\\n        parseable = call[\\\"parseable\\\"]\\n\\n        # Normalized gain: relative loss reduction attributed to the tool call\\n        if loss_without == 0.0:\\n            normalized_gain = 0.0\\n        else:\\n            normalized_gain = (loss_without - loss_with) / loss_without\\n\\n        # Accept only if the response was parseable and gain meets the threshold\\n        accepted = parseable and (normalized_gain \u003e= threshold)\\n\\n        diagnostics.append({\\n            \\\"call_id\\\": call_id,\\n            \\\"normalized_gain\\\": normalized_gain,\\n            \\\"accepted\\\": accepted\\n        })\\n\\n        if accepted:\\n            accepted_call_ids.append(call_id)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"accepted_call_ids\\\": accepted_call_ids,\\n        \\\"diagnostics\\\": diagnostics\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01whAqxXyGUumu2o95CceRJK","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2111},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2111,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":98,"output_tokens":3122,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2827},"service_tier":"standard"}}